1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,800
On the 7th of May 2022,

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:06,440
the ice-dammed lake below
Shisper glacier broke through.

3
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:09,560
Its flood tore down the Hassanabad gorge,

4
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:12,400
and the Karakoram Highway bridge was gone.

5
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:15,840
Across Gilgit-Baltistan
and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa,

6
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:19,700
more than three thousand
glacial lakes fill every summer.

7
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:21,900
Some of them could do the same.

8
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:24,820
Pakistan's space agency, SUPARCO,

9
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:28,480
tracks one hundred and thirty-one
of them as potentially dangerous,

10
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:30,560
mostly with optical satellites.

11
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:34,640
But in summer, when the lakes
fill, the clouds come, too.

12
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:38,970
Its own update in July said cloud
cover after the 16th of June

13
00:00:39,010 --> 00:00:41,380
made lake changes difficult to assess.

14
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:43,340
Over Attabad Lake alone,

15
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:47,740
five of thirteen summer Sentinel-2
images were mostly cloud.

16
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:50,640
Radar doesn't wait for clear skies.

17
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:53,920
NISAR, the new satellite
from NASA and ISRO,

18
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:57,580
sends L-band microwaves down
and listens for the echo.

19
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:01,340
Rough ground scatters the
energy back, so it looks bright.

20
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:05,300
Calm water acts like a mirror
and bounces the signal away,

21
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:09,920
so a lake looks dark, day
or night, cloud or clear.

22
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:12,500
But mountains cast shadows, too.

23
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:14,360
Radar looks sideways,

24
00:01:14,410 --> 00:01:18,210
so a slope facing away from the
satellite falls into radar shadow,

25
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:20,360
and it looks just as dark as water.

26
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:23,840
NISAR passes over each
lake in two directions:

27
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:26,240
once heading north, once heading south.

28
00:01:26,490 --> 00:01:28,680
A lake is dark from both sides.

29
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:31,060
A shadow is dark from only one.

30
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:33,320
So SARtale compares the two,

31
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:36,600
and anything dark from
just one side is set aside.

32
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:41,340
For every pass, SARtale reads a
six-kilometre window around the lake

33
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:44,840
straight from NASA's archive
without downloading the whole file.

34
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:46,480
It calms the speckle,

35
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:49,530
then marks every pixel at
least three decibels darker

36
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:52,660
than the rest of the window
in both polarisations.

37
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:56,920
Inside the lake's outline, those
pixels become a water area.

38
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:02,260
On the 23rd of October, Attabad held
two point zero eight square kilometres.

39
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:03,940
But is that a change?

40
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:05,760
One number can't tell you,

41
00:02:05,970 --> 00:02:09,550
so each lake is compared only
with its own earlier passes

42
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:13,440
from the same track because a different
angle sees the lake differently.

43
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:16,710
The spread of those passes
is the lake's own noise,

44
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:18,570
and only a change bigger than twice

45
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:21,160
that noise counts as growing or shrinking.

46
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:23,510
For Attabad, the change was a quarter

47
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:27,440
of a square kilometre against
a noise of half. Stable.

48
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:29,270
And a pass that looks wrong,

49
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:33,420
like one on the 7th of July that came
back twenty-five decibels too bright,

50
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:35,780
is left out with the reason shown.

51
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:40,380
We also checked ourselves against
Sentinel-2 on the days the sky was clear.

52
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:42,520
The agreement was poor.

53
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:44,350
At every lake we tested,

54
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:48,640
radar water and optical water
overlapped by less than one tenth.

55
00:02:49,030 --> 00:02:52,020
Neither one is a perfect
reference, and the app says so.

56
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:56,500
This is indicative screening on
provisional data, not a warning.

57
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:01,620
Warnings come from PMD,
NDMA, GBDMA, and PDMA.

58
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:05,210
In the app, every lake
gets one plain status,

59
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:08,560
a chart of every pass, and
a one-page brief to print.

60
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:11,600
And anyone can put forward a
lake we aren't watching yet.

61
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:17,360
Moments later, SARtale asks NASA's
catalogue how often NISAR passes over it.

62
00:03:17,670 --> 00:03:20,190
From the 17th of June
to the 4th of August,

63
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:22,300
while clouds limited optical checks,

64
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:26,060
NISAR gave us sixty valid
looks across eleven lakes.

65
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,240
That is the promise of radar.

66
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:31,520
When clouds close in,
the lakes stay in view.

67
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:38,000
SARtale by Team SARcastic Scientists
for NASA Space Apps, Islamabad.
